      <h1>Is Trulicity Insulin? What Dulaglutide Actually Is (and Isn't)</h1>
      <p class="article-deck">It comes in a pen, you inject it, and it treats diabetes — so plenty of people assume Trulicity is insulin. It isn't. Here's what dulaglutide actually does, and where it fits in the GLP-1 family.</p>

      <div class="tldr-box">
        <h2>TL;DR</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>No, Trulicity is not insulin.</strong> It's dulaglutide, a GLP-1 receptor agonist — the same drug class as Ozempic and part of the same family as Mounjaro.</li>
          <li>It's a <strong>once-weekly injection licensed for type 2 diabetes</strong>. It is not licensed for weight loss, and it's not used for type 1 diabetes on its own.</li>
          <li>It works by prompting your own body to release insulin <em>when blood sugar is high</em>, slowing digestion, and reducing appetite.</li>
          <li>Weight loss on Trulicity is real but modest — noticeably less than semaglutide or tirzepatide.</li>
          <li>Some people take Trulicity <em>alongside</em> insulin — which is probably where the confusion comes from.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p>Trulicity is the brand name for <strong>dulaglutide</strong>, made by Eli Lilly — the same company behind Mounjaro. It belongs to the GLP-1 receptor agonist class, not the insulin family. The confusion is understandable: both come in injection pens, both treat type 2 diabetes, and both lower blood sugar. But they do it in completely different ways.</p>

      <h2>Insulin vs a GLP-1: the actual difference</h2>
      <p><strong>Insulin</strong> is a replacement hormone. You inject it, it enters your bloodstream, and it directly pushes glucose out of your blood and into your cells. Dose it wrong and your blood sugar can drop dangerously low — hypoglycaemia is a constant consideration.</p>
      <p><strong>Dulaglutide</strong> works upstream. It mimics GLP-1, a gut hormone your body already makes, and essentially nudges your own pancreas to release insulin — but only in response to elevated blood sugar. It's glucose-dependent, which is why a GLP-1 on its own very rarely causes hypoglycaemia. It also slows stomach emptying and dials down appetite, the effects the whole class is now famous for.</p>
      <p>One drug replaces a hormone; the other coaches your body to use its own better. That's the whole distinction.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Where the mix-up deepens:</strong> Trulicity is often prescribed <em>in combination with</em> insulin when type 2 diabetes needs more than one line of treatment. Seeing both pens in the same fridge, it's easy to file them as the same kind of thing. They're teammates, not twins.
      </div>

      <h2>What Trulicity is used for</h2>
      <p>Dulaglutide is licensed in the UK for <strong>type 2 diabetes</strong> — to improve blood sugar control alongside diet and exercise, either on its own (when metformin isn't suitable) or added to other diabetes medications. Its trials also showed cardiovascular benefit, reducing the risk of major events like heart attack and stroke in people with type 2 diabetes and cardiovascular risk factors, which is part of why it became so widely prescribed.</p>
      <p>It's taken <strong>once a week</strong>, from a single-use auto-injector pen that hides the needle — a design many needle-phobic users genuinely rate. Doses range from 0.75mg up to 4.5mg weekly.</p>

      <h3>What it's not used for</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Weight loss.</strong> Unlike semaglutide (Wegovy) and tirzepatide (Mounjaro), dulaglutide has no weight-management licence anywhere. People on it for diabetes do tend to lose some weight — typically a few kilograms — but it's a side benefit, not the job, and it's clearly the weakest of the modern GLP-1s on this front.</li>
        <li><strong>Type 1 diabetes.</strong> Type 1 requires insulin, full stop; a GLP-1 can't replace it.</li>
        <li><strong>Prediabetes.</strong> A question people search for, but it's not licensed for this. Prediabetes management runs through lifestyle programmes (and occasionally metformin), not Trulicity.</li>
      </ul>

