      <h2>Side effects</h2>
      <p>Classic GLP-1 territory: nausea, diarrhoea or constipation, reduced appetite, and occasional injection-site reactions, mostly during the first weeks or after a dose increase. Because dulaglutide's appetite effect is gentler than the newer drugs, many long-term users describe it as the easiest GLP-1 they've been on. The class-wide cautions — pancreatitis, gallbladder problems, and avoiding it with a personal or family history of medullary thyroid cancer — apply here too. Our <a href="/managing-glp1-nausea">nausea guide</a> covers the practical side.</p>

      <h2>The bottom line</h2>
      <p>Trulicity is a weekly GLP-1 injection for type 2 diabetes — a cousin of Ozempic and Mounjaro, not a form of insulin. If you've been prescribed it, you're on an appetite-and-blood-sugar drug with a long track record and a famously friendly pen. And if you arrived here hoping it was a weight-loss option: it isn't licensed for that, and its stablemate Mounjaro is the drug Lilly built for the job. For the full pharmacology, see our <a href="/dulaglutide">dulaglutide reference page</a>.</p>

